 "result": "This repo is a renamed Flask (\"Tessel\"). The two mechanisms below are what make `request`, `session` and `g` available in every template.\n\n**1. Jinja globals, set once on the environment**\n- `create_jinja_environment()` in `src/tessel/app.py:467` calls `rv.globals.update(...)` at line 493. That adds the `request`, `session` and `g` proxies, along with `config`, `link_for` and `get_flashed_messages`.\n- Because they are globals, they also work in imported or included templates, which don't receive the render context.\n\n**2. Per-render context injection**\n- `render_template` and `render_template_string` both call `_render()` (`src/tessel/templating.py:123`). That calls `app.fill_template_scope(ctx, context)` (`src/tessel/app.py:588`).\n- `fill_template_scope` runs every function in `template_ctx_injectors`: first the app-wide ones (key `None`), then the ones for the current request's bundles. It merges their returned dicts into the context, then re-applies the values passed to `render_template`, so those always take precedence.\n- The built-in injector is `_base_template_vars()` (`src/tessel/templating.py:21`). It is pre-registered in `Scaffold.__init__` (`src/tessel/sansio/scaffold.py:184`). It swaps the `g` and `request` proxies for the concrete objects, which is faster. `session` stays a proxy so that reading it still marks the session as accessed.\n\n**Adding your own variables**\n- Use the `@app.ctx_injector` decorator (`Scaffold.ctx_injector`, `src/tessel/sansio/scaffold.py:550`). The function returns a dict that is merged into the context:\n  ```python\n  @app.ctx_injector\n  def inject_user():\n      return {\"user\": current_user}\n  ```\n- On a bundle, `@bundle.ctx_injector` applies only to that bundle's views. `@bundle.app_ctx_injector` (`src/tessel/sansio/bundles.py:644`) applies to every template.\n- Alternatively, add entries directly to `app.jinja_env.globals`.",
